1
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:04,860
Forty-seven billion dollars.

2
00:00:05,877 --> 00:00:10,165
That's what investors reportedly
valued WeWork at in 2019.

3
00:00:10,859 --> 00:00:13,489
Not a company manufacturing
breakthrough technology.

4
00:00:14,079 --> 00:00:17,289
Not a company selling software
to millions of users.

5
00:00:17,827 --> 00:00:22,660
A company renting office space,
redesigning it, and renting it out again.

6
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:25,949
Its founder promised to change
the way the world worked.

7
00:00:26,416 --> 00:00:30,294
Four years later, WeWork was
filing for bankruptcy protection.

8
00:00:30,765 --> 00:00:33,822
So how did a forty-seven-billion-dollar
dream unravel?

9
00:00:35,783 --> 00:00:38,654
The bankruptcy filing wasn't
the beginning of the story.

10
00:00:39,064 --> 00:00:40,974
It was the result of years of decisions.

11
00:00:41,715 --> 00:00:47,047
WeWork offered something customers genuinely
wanted: flexible, attractive offices

12
00:00:47,377 --> 00:00:50,487
without the burden of building
an entire workplace themselves.

13
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:54,351
But beneath that promise was
a financial contradiction.

14
00:00:55,031 --> 00:01:00,395
Its customers wanted flexibility. Its
landlords wanted long-term commitments.

15
00:01:01,103 --> 00:01:03,316
And WeWork stood between them.

16
00:01:03,687 --> 00:01:08,554
To understand the collapse, we need to
examine what the company was selling, what it

17
00:01:08,594 --> 00:01:11,324
was spending, and what its
own documents revealed.

18
00:01:13,186 --> 00:01:19,617
When WeWork opened its first location in
New York in 2010, the idea was compelling.

19
00:01:19,729 --> 00:01:24,574
Traditional office space often required a
business to negotiate a lease, furnish the

20
00:01:24,614 --> 00:01:29,254
premises, arrange services and commit
capital before employees could even begin

21
00:01:29,294 --> 00:01:30,294
working.

22
00:01:30,425 --> 00:01:32,486
WeWork offered an alternative.

23
00:01:33,055 --> 00:01:38,694
Desks, meeting rooms, shared spaces and a
recognizable design language were bundled

24
00:01:38,734 --> 00:01:41,084
into a more convenient experience.

25
00:01:41,652 --> 00:01:46,177
For startups, freelancers and expanding
companies, the appeal was real.

26
00:01:46,637 --> 00:01:50,243
This wasn't a business
built entirely on hype.

27
00:01:50,873 --> 00:01:53,223
There was a useful product underneath it.

28
00:01:54,756 --> 00:01:59,013
But under co-founder Adam Neumann, WeWork
became more than an office-space business

29
00:01:59,053 --> 00:02:00,723
in its own telling.

30
00:02:01,264 --> 00:02:05,593
It described a global community, a
network and a new way of working.

31
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:08,543
Expansion became central to the story.

32
00:02:08,994 --> 00:02:13,077
More buildings meant more cities.
More cities meant more members.

33
00:02:13,267 --> 00:02:17,517
And more members seemed to promise
an increasingly valuable platform.

34
00:02:18,128 --> 00:02:21,177
That narrative attracted
enormous amounts of capital.

35
00:02:21,766 --> 00:02:26,299
Yet the larger WeWork became, the
more important one question grew.

36
00:02:26,712 --> 00:02:29,327
Could the economics support the ambition?

37
00:02:31,044 --> 00:02:35,112
Strip away the branding, and WeWork's
core business was surprisingly

38
00:02:35,152 --> 00:02:36,322
straightforward.

39
00:02:36,938 --> 00:02:42,028
It signed leases with property owners. It
spent money transforming those properties

40
00:02:42,068 --> 00:02:48,528
into attractive workspaces. Then it sold
access to those spaces through memberships

41
00:02:48,568 --> 00:02:49,918
and office agreements.

42
00:02:50,612 --> 00:02:55,502
The opportunity was clear. A busy location
could generate substantial revenue.

43
00:02:56,034 --> 00:03:01,145
But the company had to commit to rent,
construction, furniture and staffing before

44
00:03:01,185 --> 00:03:03,235
the building reached its potential.

45
00:03:03,638 --> 00:03:08,881
Every new location required investment.
And every new lease created another

46
00:03:08,921 --> 00:03:09,921
obligation.

47
00:03:11,576 --> 00:03:13,718
Here's where the risk becomes clearer.

48
00:03:14,220 --> 00:03:17,837
Many WeWork customers could commit
for relatively short periods.

49
00:03:18,567 --> 00:03:22,377
The company itself often signed
leases lasting many years.

50
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:26,323
When demand was strong, the
difference could be managed.

51
00:03:26,897 --> 00:03:31,373
When demand weakened, customers could
leave faster than WeWork could reduce its

52
00:03:31,413 --> 00:03:32,523
obligations.

53
00:03:33,443 --> 00:03:37,116
Rent still had to be paid.
Buildings still needed maintenance.

54
00:03:37,706 --> 00:03:40,316
And unused space still carried costs.

55
00:03:40,956 --> 00:03:43,357
The problem wasn't flexibility itself.

56
00:03:44,021 --> 00:03:46,669
It was who carried the
risk of providing it.

57
00:03:48,256 --> 00:03:54,586
By June 2019, WeWork had disclosed
approximately thirty-four billion dollars in

58
00:03:54,626 --> 00:03:58,856
future minimum payments on operating
leases that had already commenced.

59
00:03:59,515 --> 00:04:04,448
These were undiscounted payments spread over
future years, not a single bill payable

60
00:04:04,488 --> 00:04:05,488
tomorrow.

61
00:04:05,818 --> 00:04:10,507
And there were additional commitments
for leases signed but not yet started.

62
00:04:11,038 --> 00:04:14,458
The numbers revealed the scale
of the company's expansion.

63
00:04:14,975 --> 00:04:20,102
They also revealed how much of its future
depended on keeping buildings occupied and

64
00:04:20,142 --> 00:04:21,142
revenue flowing.

65
00:04:22,906 --> 00:04:27,052
On August fourteenth,
2019, the story changed.

66
00:04:27,599 --> 00:04:31,651
WeWork's parent company filed its
registration statement for an initial public

67
00:04:31,691 --> 00:04:32,781
offering.

68
00:04:32,881 --> 00:04:37,490
For years, investors had been hearing about
community, growth and transformation.

69
00:04:38,182 --> 00:04:42,298
Now the company was asking public-market
investors to examine its financial

70
00:04:42,338 --> 00:04:43,488
statements.

71
00:04:44,045 --> 00:04:47,265
And inside those documents were
the details that mattered.

72
00:04:47,732 --> 00:04:52,542
Revenue. Losses. Lease commitments.
Governance arrangements.

73
00:04:53,315 --> 00:04:58,175
The company's own disclosures made it
possible to test the promise against the

74
00:04:58,215 --> 00:04:59,215
economics.

75
00:05:00,792 --> 00:05:06,381
In 2018, WeWork reported roughly one
point eight billion dollars in revenue.

76
00:05:06,874 --> 00:05:11,559
It also reported a net loss of
approximately one point six billion.

77
00:05:12,045 --> 00:05:15,785
The company was expanding rapidly,
but the losses were substantial.

78
00:05:16,414 --> 00:05:19,857
That didn't automatically mean
the business could never work.

79
00:05:20,283 --> 00:05:24,045
High-growth companies often spend
heavily before reaching profitability.

80
00:05:24,782 --> 00:05:30,078
The question was whether WeWork's economics
would improve as it expanded, or whether

81
00:05:30,118 --> 00:05:33,108
expansion would keep
demanding more capital.

82
00:05:34,828 --> 00:05:36,727
The filing raised another question.

83
00:05:37,181 --> 00:05:41,410
Who was benefiting from the company's
success, and who controlled its decisions?

84
00:05:42,104 --> 00:05:47,869
One disclosed transaction involved
trademarks associated with the word "We."

85
00:05:48,343 --> 00:05:52,233
An entity connected to Adam Neumann had
received interests valued at approximately

86
00:05:52,273 --> 00:05:57,543
five point nine million dollars
in exchange for those rights.

87
00:05:58,159 --> 00:06:00,308
The arrangement was later unwound.

88
00:06:00,838 --> 00:06:05,649
Meanwhile, the proposed voting structure
gave Neumann extraordinary influence.

89
00:06:06,257 --> 00:06:09,239
Investors weren't just
evaluating a business model.

90
00:06:09,717 --> 00:06:11,870
They were evaluating its governance.

91
00:06:13,761 --> 00:06:16,467
The planned IPO began to unravel.

92
00:06:16,970 --> 00:06:21,915
Investors questioned the valuation, the
losses, and the governance structure.

93
00:06:22,373 --> 00:06:25,610
The original investment
story was no longer enough.

94
00:06:26,168 --> 00:06:31,271
In September 2019, Adam Neumann
stepped down as chief executive.

95
00:06:31,922 --> 00:06:36,665
On September thirtieth, WeWork formally
withdrew its IPO registration.

96
00:06:37,454 --> 00:06:42,506
A company that had spent years pursuing
rapid expansion was suddenly confronting a

97
00:06:42,546 --> 00:06:43,956
much harder reality.

98
00:06:44,406 --> 00:06:47,069
Its next challenge wasn't growing faster.

99
00:06:47,626 --> 00:06:50,397
It was securing the
capital needed to survive.

100
00:06:52,357 --> 00:06:55,660
SoftBank stepped in with a
major financing package.

101
00:06:56,183 --> 00:07:01,006
WeWork began cutting costs, restructuring
operations and reconsidering its property

102
00:07:01,046 --> 00:07:02,056
commitments.

103
00:07:02,793 --> 00:07:06,968
Two years later, it finally reached the
public market through a merger with a

104
00:07:07,008 --> 00:07:09,098
special-purpose acquisition company.

105
00:07:09,522 --> 00:07:14,498
The announced transaction carried an initial
enterprise value of approximately nine

106
00:07:14,538 --> 00:07:15,948
billion dollars.

107
00:07:16,815 --> 00:07:21,473
That wasn't directly comparable to
the earlier private equity valuation.

108
00:07:21,513 --> 00:07:25,836
But the change in investor
expectations was unmistakable.

109
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:28,119
The company was still operating.

110
00:07:28,651 --> 00:07:30,506
The dream had been repriced.

111
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:33,801
Then came another challenge.

112
00:07:34,426 --> 00:07:39,450
In 2020, the pandemic disrupted
office demand around the world.

113
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:44,405
Companies experimented with remote work.
Tenants reconsidered how much space they

114
00:07:44,445 --> 00:07:48,535
needed. And uncertainty made
long-term planning more difficult.

115
00:07:49,088 --> 00:07:54,188
For WeWork, already attempting a major
restructuring, the timing was punishing.

116
00:07:54,749 --> 00:07:59,606
The company negotiated lease changes,
exited locations and tried to reduce its

117
00:07:59,646 --> 00:08:00,646
commitments.

118
00:08:01,216 --> 00:08:04,982
But restructuring a global
property portfolio takes time.

119
00:08:05,531 --> 00:08:08,398
And time was becoming
increasingly expensive.

120
00:08:10,313 --> 00:08:14,544
By 2023, the warnings had become explicit.

121
00:08:15,111 --> 00:08:20,426
WeWork disclosed substantial doubt about
its ability to continue as a going concern.

122
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:26,281
Losses, member churn and liquidity pressure
had narrowed the company's options.

123
00:08:26,881 --> 00:08:30,651
At the end of September, it reported
approximately four hundred and sixty-two

124
00:08:30,691 --> 00:08:34,001
million dollars in cash
and cash equivalents.

125
00:08:34,672 --> 00:08:39,078
On November sixth, WeWork and certain
subsidiaries filed for Chapter Eleven

126
00:08:39,118 --> 00:08:41,288
bankruptcy protection.

127
00:08:41,789 --> 00:08:44,769
The growth story had reached
its financial reckoning.

128
00:08:46,424 --> 00:08:50,549
But bankruptcy did not mean the
disappearance of every WeWork office.

129
00:08:51,206 --> 00:08:54,349
The company continued operating
through the restructuring process.

130
00:08:55,046 --> 00:09:01,367
On June eleventh, 2024, it emerged from
Chapter Eleven with a reorganized business

131
00:09:01,407 --> 00:09:04,457
and a reduced portfolio of obligations.

132
00:09:05,230 --> 00:09:07,019
The service still existed.

133
00:09:07,661 --> 00:09:10,443
Customers could still use WeWork spaces.

134
00:09:11,052 --> 00:09:16,671
What had collapsed was the original
investment narrative that once supported a

135
00:09:16,711 --> 00:09:19,301
forty-seven-billion-dollar
private valuation.

136
00:09:19,941 --> 00:09:20,974
The product survived.

137
00:09:21,732 --> 00:09:23,815
The financial story had changed.

138
00:09:25,646 --> 00:09:29,509
So what happened to WeWork's
forty-seven-billion-dollar dream?

139
00:09:30,003 --> 00:09:31,660
It wasn't one mistake.

140
00:09:32,193 --> 00:09:36,536
It was the combination of expensive
expansion, long-term lease commitments,

141
00:09:36,576 --> 00:09:42,066
governance concerns, and a business
exposed to changing demand.

142
00:09:42,672 --> 00:09:45,925
WeWork proved that customers
wanted flexible offices.

143
00:09:46,602 --> 00:09:51,428
But a useful product and a sustainable
investment are not the same thing.

144
00:09:51,828 --> 00:09:55,223
The warning signs were visible in
the company's own disclosures.

145
00:09:55,937 --> 00:09:58,117
And that is the final lesson.

146
00:09:58,587 --> 00:10:00,398
Every brand leaves evidence.

